---
scope: src/service/install
status: aligned
description: >
  Install service — the consumer-agnostic core that turns a cold project into an
  AIDE-ready project, shared by the `aide_init` MCP tool and the npx CLI. Writes
  non-existing files silently, only pausing on overwrites or inherently
  interactive decisions (brain vault path, MCP config merge, IDE choice).
intent: >
  The install service is the shared core that turns a cold project
  into an AIDE-ready project. It is consumer-agnostic: the same
  service is invoked by the `aide_init` MCP tool (when the user is
  talking to an agent connected to this server) and by the npx CLI
  (when the user has nothing connected yet). The two consumers
  differ only in their wire shape; the planning, idempotency,
  prescriptions, and category structure live here. The service is
  designed to be fast and unobtrusive: when a category has nothing
  on disk to overwrite, it just writes the files and reports what it
  did. The consumer pauses the user only when the install would
  destroy or clobber something they already have, or when a decision
  cannot be resolved from the filesystem alone.

  The rule is: confirmation is reserved for overwrites and for
  inherent decisions. A category whose plan contains only would-create
  steps runs silently and reports its results. A category whose plan
  contains would-overwrite steps pauses and asks the user — category-
  by-category, using the consumer's native pause shape — before
  applying. Inherent decisions — framework detection confirmation,
  brain vault location, MCP server-merge into an existing config, and
  IDE choice — always prompt the user regardless of disk state,
  because filesystem contents cannot resolve them.

  The categories are: framework detection, methodology pointer stub,
  methodology doc hub, pipeline slash commands, pipeline agent
  definitions, pipeline skill templates, MCP server registration
  (aide), brain vault provisioning, MCP server registration
  (brain), and IDE configuration. The brain MCP entry is keyed
  `brain` per the project-root settlement; the entry's command/args
  shape identifies the wired backend (obsidian today, others tomorrow).
  Each category still reports what it would do (create, overwrite,
  skip, already exists), and the consumer only surfaces the category
  to the user when an overwrite is pending or an inherent decision is
  needed.

  The brain layer is not optional — AIDE is opinionated about this.
  The pipeline needs a brain to persist research and retros. But the
  user chooses where it lives: they can connect to an existing vault
  anywhere on their system, or have the install create a new one at a
  location they specify. The consumer interviews the user to resolve
  the vault path; the install service never silently assumes a sibling
  folder or falls back to a default. This interview happens regardless
  of disk state because the vault path is an inherent decision, not a
  file-write decision.

  MCP config changes are returned as prescriptions — the key name and
  server entry the config needs — not written directly by the install
  service. The consumer reads the existing MCP config, merges the
  prescribed entry into it, and writes the result. The user sees the
  merge before it happens. This ensures the consumer has full
  visibility into what exists in the config and the user's other
  servers are never at risk, even when the config is malformed.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The install service returns a structured plan containing a
      categorized list of steps, each with a status (would-create,
      would-overwrite, would-skip, exists) and the data the consumer
      needs to execute that step (file paths, content, MCP entries).
      The consumer interprets this plan and decides per-category
      whether user interaction is needed. Same plan shape regardless
      of which entry point invoked the service.
    - Framework auto-detection picks the right framework from files
      on disk, and the consumer confirms the detection with the user
      before proceeding. Framework choice is an inherent decision —
      the prompt happens regardless of disk state.
    - "A category whose plan contains only would-create steps (no
      existing files on disk) is applied silently by the consumer:
      no confirmation, just write and report. The install is
      intentionally fast for the common cold-start path where there
      is nothing to preserve."
    - "A category whose plan contains at least one would-overwrite
      step pauses the consumer category-by-category using its
      native structured-prompt mechanism (AskUserQuestion in the
      MCP tool path, stdin prompts in the CLI path). The consumer
      names the files that would be overwritten so the user can
      make an informed choice, then either applies the whole
      category or skips it per the user's selection."
    - "Inherent decisions — brain vault location, MCP server merge
      into an existing config, and IDE choice — always prompt the
      user regardless of what is on disk. These are not file-write
      decisions; filesystem state cannot resolve them. The fast-path
      rule applies only to pure file-create categories."
    - For MCP config, the install service returns a prescription —
      the server key name and the entry object — instead of writing
      the file. The consumer reads the existing config, shows the
      user the planned merge, and writes. If the config is malformed
      JSON, the consumer tells the user and asks how to proceed
      rather than silently clobbering.
    - "The brain vault interview is consumer-driven and always
      happens. The consumer asks the user where their vault is,
      offering three paths: discover an existing vault (from env
      var, known locations, or user input), create a new vault at a
      specified location, or connect to an existing vault at a
      user-provided path. The vault is always provisioned — there is
      no skip-brain option. Once the path is resolved, the install
      service reports whether the vault needs scaffolding and the
      consumer applies it."
    - "Wiring the brain end-to-end from a resolved brainPath requires
      consumers to invoke the install service twice with distinct
      category values: once with \"category: mcp\" to apply the brain
      MCP prescription to .mcp.json (under the fixed `brain` key, with
      the resolved backend's command/args underneath), and once with
      \"category: brain\" to scaffold vault directories and seed
      content files. The split is intentional and load-bearing: the
      mcp category is prescription-only (the consumer merges the
      returned entry into .mcp.json because the install service
      never writes that file directly), while the brain category is
      direct filesystem work (the install service creates vault
      directories and seeds content itself). Neither category alone
      fully wires the brain — both must run for the brain to be
      reachable by pipeline agents."
    - IDE configuration is presented as optional — the consumer asks
      whether the user wants Zed and/or VS Code configuration, and
      only applies what they confirm. IDE choice is an inherent
      decision and always prompts.
    - Every step is idempotent. Re-running the install action on a
      fully initialized project — through either entry point —
      returns a plan where every step shows "exists" and the
      consumer reports that nothing needs to change, without asking
      a single question.
    - The install service's response is structured data the consumer
      interprets, not a pre-formatted summary the consumer parrots.
      The consumer composes its own output (conversational for the
      MCP tool, terminal-friendly for the CLI) from the structured
      data, tailored to the user's context.
    - "The default response (no category parameter) is small enough
      for the consumer to comprehend in a single read — metadata
      only, no file content — and includes overwrite status per
      category so the consumer can decide which categories need
      prompts. The MCP-tool consumer is the primary driver of the
      no-content-in-default-response rule because the agent reading
      the response has a token budget; the CLI consumer benefits
      from the same shape because re-fetching content per category
      keeps streaming output coherent."
    - When called with a `category` parameter, the install service
      writes all `would-create` files directly to disk itself and
      returns a small manifest — an array of steps with `filePath`,
      `status` (now `"created"`, `"overwritten"`, or `"exists"`),
      and `name`, but NO `content` field. The consumer never sees
      file content and never needs to write new files itself. This
      eliminates the 50-100KB response problem that caused MCP-tool
      agents to resort to programmatic parsing via Node scripts
      instead of conversational interaction.
    - The consumer only writes files itself for content that
      requires intelligent merging — specifically MCP config, where
      the consumer must read the existing config, merge
      prescriptions, and write. For MCP steps, the category call
      still returns the prescription data (not file content) so the
      consumer can perform the merge. Brain vault scaffolding
      (creating directories) is handled by the install service
      directly.
    - IDE steps that require external tooling (e.g., VS Code
      extension install via CLI) return instructions for the
      consumer to execute, since those aren't simple file writes.
  undesired:
    - An install that prompts the user when there is nothing to
      overwrite. A category whose plan is entirely would-create
      steps must be applied silently — asking permission to write
      files that do not yet exist is the exact friction this
      service exists to remove, regardless of which consumer drives
      the run.
    - An install that silently overwrites existing files without
      asking. The fast path is for creates only; overwrites are
      destructive and must pause the consumer so the user can
      consent category-by-category.
    - "A category that bundles creates and overwrites and silently
      applies both because most of its files are new. If any file
      in the category would overwrite, the category pauses — the
      user is informed about the overwrite and decides whether to
      apply the whole category."
    - An install that asks conversational free-text questions
      instead of using the consumer's native structured-prompt
      mechanism for the overwrite and inherent-decision paths.
      Every pause point must use defined labels the user can
      select — `AskUserQuestion` in the MCP-tool path, structured
      stdin prompts in the CLI path — not open-ended text prompts.
    - An install that skips the inherent-decision prompts (brain
      vault path, MCP config merge, IDE choice) because the
      fast-path rule was misapplied. Those prompts are not about
      file-writes and must always happen.
    - An install that silently assumes a brain vault path from a
      sibling folder, environment variable, or platform default
      without asking the user. Discovery hints are useful for
      suggesting a default, but the consumer must confirm the path
      with the user.
    - An install service that writes MCP config entries directly,
      bypassing the consumer. The prescription pattern exists so
      the consumer has full visibility and the user's existing
      servers are never at risk.
    - An install that allows skipping the brain entirely. AIDE
      requires a brain — research and retros depend on it. The
      user chooses where, not whether.
    - "A consumer invoking only \"category: brain\" with a resolved
      brainPath and declaring the brain fully wired. That single
      invocation scaffolds the vault directories and seeds content
      but leaves the brain MCP prescription unapplied — the
      .mcp.json vaultPath stays empty, pipeline agents that declare
      mcpServers: [brain] cannot reach the vault, and aide_info
      will still report \"brain.status: invalid-path\" even though
      the vault exists on disk. The symmetric failure also applies:
      a consumer invoking only \"category: mcp\" with a brainPath
      patches .mcp.json but never scaffolds the vault, leaving the
      brain MCP server pointed at an unprovisioned directory. Both
      categories must run; treating either alone as sufficient
      produces a half-wired brain."
    - An install that clobbers the MCP config when it encounters
      malformed JSON. The consumer handles this case by asking the
      user, not by overwriting.
    - An install service that returns a pre-formatted prose summary
      instead of structured data. Both consumers need structured
      data to drive their flow — not a string they have to parse or
      parrot.
    - A category call that returns full file content for the
      consumer to write. The category call must write files to disk
      itself and return only a manifest. Returning content causes
      MCP-tool agents to receive 13-50KB responses that get
      persisted to disk as JSON files, which the agent then tries
      to parse with Node scripts instead of using the Write tool
      per file.
    - An install response that embeds full file content for all
      steps in a single payload. Even when steps are structured,
      a 50-100KB blob causes MCP-tool agents to write Python
      scripts to parse it instead of driving the install
      interactively. Content must be deferred to category-specific
      follow-up calls.
    - An install that appends the pointer stub on every run, fails
      the whole run on a single step failure, hardcodes vault
      paths, or reaches for the network — regardless of which entry
      point invoked it. The idempotency, fail-soft, no-network, and
      no-hardcoded-path invariants live at the install-service
      layer so both consumers inherit them identically.
    - "A new install category added without a corresponding upgrade
      category. The upgrade surface must mirror the install surface
      — every category the install action installs must have an
      upgrade path. The two category lists (InitCategory and
      UpgradeCategory) are currently independent; adding to one
      without the other creates silent drift."
    - "A standalone CLI entry point that teaches AIDE doctrine
      instead of installing. The npx CLI is permitted because it
      is a thin invocation of channel two, exposing the same
      install service the `aide_init` MCP tool exposes; a CLI that
      walks the user through the methodology interactively would
      be the forbidden third channel from the trunk spec, not a
      thin invocation of channel two."
---

## Context

Every new project that adopts AIDE has to cross the same cold-start gap:
an entry-point pointer to AIDE in the surface the agent already reads on
every session, the full methodology installed somewhere the agent can
crawl on demand, pipeline slash commands scaffolded, pipeline agent
definitions installed, pipeline skill templates installed, the MCP
server registered, the brain layer provisioned so pipeline agents can
persist and retrieve domain knowledge, and the IDE taught to render
`.aide` as Markdown. Without a single install action that does all of
this, each project rediscovers the list and gets one or two steps
wrong — a stub written to the wrong file, a doc hub installed to a path
the stub doesn't name, a slash command with a typo, an MCP config that
registers the server under the wrong name, no vault for the research
agent to write to, or an IDE that still renders `.aide` as plain text.
This service exists to collapse the cold-start gap into one
invocation, regardless of which entry point invoked it, so the decision
to adopt AIDE in a new project is trivially reversible and costs
nothing to try. The `aide_init` MCP tool is the entry point an agent
calls when the user is already talking to a model that connected to
this server; the npx CLI is the entry point a developer runs when they
have nothing connected yet. Both compile down to the same install
service contract.

The previous consumer-driven design corrected the biggest failure mode
of the original monolithic shape — silent overwrites of malformed MCP
configs, vaults silently created at sibling defaults — by pausing the
consumer category-by-category and asking the user to confirm each one
before writing. That was the right instinct for destructive writes. It
was the wrong tax for creates. The observed friction in cold-start
runs is a user watching the consumer ask "can I create `.aide/docs/`?
can I create `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`? can I create
`.claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md`?" for seven consecutive
categories of files that do not yet exist — nothing to preserve,
nothing to clobber, no decision the user can meaningfully make beyond
"yes, I ran the install, apply it." Every one of those prompts is
bureaucratic overhead on a path where the user has already committed.

This spec refines the confirmation model by splitting the install
service's decisions into two kinds. **File-write decisions** are resolvable from disk
state: the tool can stat every target path and know with certainty
whether a given category would create new files, overwrite existing
ones, or do nothing. When every step in a category would create
(nothing on disk, nothing to preserve), the category has no decision
the user can inform — the right answer is to apply it silently.
**Inherent decisions** are the ones disk state cannot resolve:
framework choice (two frameworks can coexist on disk and the user
alone knows which one the project actually targets), brain vault
location (the user is the only one who knows where their knowledge
lives), whether to merge the AIDE servers into their existing MCP
config (the user's other servers are in that file), and which IDEs
they actually want configured (tooling they don't use is noise). These
always prompt, regardless of what is on disk, because the question
cannot be answered by reading the filesystem.

The shift is behavioral, not architectural. The install service still
plans every step, still returns structured data, still executes writes
itself via apply mode when called with a category — nothing about the
two-call pattern changes for the MCP-tool consumer, and the CLI
consumer drives the same plan/apply boundary in-process instead of
across a wire. What changes is the consumer's branch logic over the
plan: a category whose plan is entirely would-create steps is applied
silently and reported after the fact; a category whose plan contains
at least one would-overwrite step pauses the consumer with the
affected files named so the user can make an informed choice about
their own existing work; inherent decisions always prompt because
they are not file-write decisions at all. A fully initialized project,
re-run through either entry point, produces a plan that is entirely
`exists` steps, zero prompts, and a terse "nothing to change" report.
A fresh project produces a plan that is entirely `would-create` file
steps plus three-or-four inherent prompts, and the user is
interrupted only for those.

Methodology delivery inside this service is split across two peer
helpers on purpose. One installs the short pointer stub into the
host's config file so the agent learns in its very first read of the
session that AIDE exists and where the full material lives. The other
installs the full material — the canonical methodology docs plus a
hub index — into a dedicated doc-hub folder on the host's disk, so
the agent reads only what the current task requires and only when the
task requires it. The split mirrors the methodology's own
progressive-disclosure rule at the delivery layer: the high-traffic
surface teaches where to look, the low-traffic surface holds the
actual content. Both land through the same silent-on-create path when
nothing of the user's is at risk.

The brain layer is the pipeline's shared memory. The research agent
writes domain findings to `research/<domain>/`, QA promotes retros to
`process/retro/`, and the coding playbook lives under
`coding-playbook/`. Without a vault and a `brain` MCP server entry,
agents that declare `mcpServers: [brain]` in their frontmatter
silently lose brain access — their research vanishes, retros are
never persisted, and the pipeline cannot learn across runs. The
install action provisions this layer: it creates a minimal vault
with the expected directory scaffolding when the user-chosen location
is empty, and prescribes the brain MCP server entry (keyed `brain`,
with the resolved backend's command/args underneath) for the consumer
to merge into the project's MCP config alongside the aide server. The vault path is resolved through the consumer's interview
with the user — never hardcoded, never silently defaulted — because
the vault path is an inherent decision and the rule that covers it is
"always prompt," not "apply silently when nothing is on disk."

## Strategy

**The install service plans with a four-status enum; the consumer
branches on status distribution per category.** Each step in the plan
reports `would-create` (target path does not exist),
`would-overwrite` (target path exists and the canonical bytes differ
from what is on disk), `would-skip` (target path exists and the
canonical bytes match, so no write is needed), or `exists` (step is
already satisfied, nothing to do). The consumer walks the plan
category by category and picks one of three branches: if the
category's steps are all `would-create` (or a mix of `would-create`,
`would-skip`, and `exists`), apply silently — there is no decision
the user can inform, and every prompt on that path is friction the
spec exists to remove. If any step in the category is
`would-overwrite`, pause and ask, naming the files so the user can
decide whether to overwrite their own modified material. This is how
the spec's outcomes.desired rule "a pure-create category is applied
silently; a category with overwrites pauses" cashes out in
consumer-visible behavior. The agent in the MCP-tool consumer drives
this branching across the wire; the CLI consumer drives it
in-process — the rule is identical.

**`would-overwrite` is computed by byte comparison, not heuristics.**
Each helper stats its target path; if the file exists, it reads the
on-disk bytes and compares them to the canonical content it was about
to write. Identical bytes ⇒ `would-skip` (the file is already what we
would have written, no need to write again). Different bytes ⇒
`would-overwrite` (the user has modified this file, or a prior
version of the install service wrote something that has since
drifted). Missing file ⇒ `would-create`. The rule is deliberately
strict: "same name but different content" is enough to pause the
consumer, because the install service cannot tell a user-authored
customization from a stale previous install, and the safer branch is
to ask. This closes the outcomes.undesired line "a category that
bundles creates and overwrites and silently applies both because most
of its files are new" — even one overwrite in a category tips the
whole category into the prompt branch.

**Inherent-decision prompts are orthogonal to the file-write
branch.** Framework confirmation, brain vault location, MCP-config
merge into an existing config, and IDE choice always pause the
consumer regardless of the plan's status distribution. The rule
"silent on create, prompt on overwrite" governs file-writes only;
inherent decisions are not file-writes, they are choices about what
the user wants their environment to look like, and the filesystem
cannot resolve them. A fully-initialized project re-run still asks
zero questions because the categories land as `exists` *and* the
inherent decisions have already been answered on disk — brain path
present in the registered MCP server entry, IDE config present,
framework already detected — so the consumer has no new questions to
ask. A fresh project asks three or four (framework confirm, brain
vault path, MCP merge approval, IDE choice) and nothing else. This
closes the outcomes.undesired line "an install that skips the
inherent-decision prompts because the fast-path rule was misapplied"
— the rule does not apply to them in the first place.

**Apply mode writes creates immediately and defers overwrites to
user consent.** When the consumer invokes the install service with a
category parameter, the apply helper walks the category's steps:
every `would-create` step is written to disk straight through and
returned with status `"created"`; `would-skip` and `exists` steps
pass through unchanged; `would-overwrite` steps are only written
when the consumer explicitly signaled user approval for the category
(the consumer does this by proceeding to the apply call — if the
user had declined, the consumer would never have made it). On
approval, the overwrite happens and the step is returned with status
`"overwritten"`. The manifest that comes back carries the new
`"overwritten"` status alongside `"created"` and `"exists"` so the
consumer can report a truthful breakdown — "created 8, overwrote 1,
kept 3 as-is" — not a vague "done." This satisfies the
outcomes.desired line about manifest statuses and the
outcomes.undesired line about silent clobbers (writes happen, but
only when the plan already showed them as overwrites and the
consumer already got approval).

**Category isolation: one category's decision never mutates
another.** Each helper (methodology, commands, agents, skills, MCP,
brain, IDE) produces its plan from its own canonical content and its
own disk inspection. No shared state, no cross-category coupling, no
ordering dependency beyond the consumer's presentation order. This
matches the orchestrator/helper pattern the codebase already uses
(`study-playbook` convention: orchestrator coordinates, helpers do
one thing) and is the forcing function that makes per-category
pauses work — the consumer can apply methodology silently, pause on
commands because the user has a modified slash command, apply agents
silently, and the categories do not contaminate each other. A
category's failure surfaces as a single category's failure in the
report; it does not abort the rest. This closes the outcomes.desired
"every step is idempotent" line at the category-boundary level too.

**MCP merge is an inherent-decision wrapper around file-write
prescription.** The install service never writes MCP config. It
returns the server key name and the entry object the config needs;
the consumer reads the existing config, shows the user the planned
merge, and writes. The "show the user before writing" pause is an
inherent-decision prompt — the user's other servers live in that
file, the install service cannot know which ones the user wants
preserved, and the filesystem state of `.mcp.json` does not answer
the question. Malformed JSON hits the same path: the consumer
surfaces the parse error and asks how to proceed rather than
silently replacing the file. This closes the outcomes.undesired
lines about clobbering MCP configs and writing MCP entries directly,
and honors the parent spec's `src/.aide` invariant that the install
service never touches MCP config.

**The brain MCP key is fixed at `brain`; legacy `obsidian` keys
migrate through `provisionBrain`.** The MCP entry the install service
prescribes is always keyed `brain`, regardless of which backend's
command/args the entry carries. Legacy installs that wrote the entry
under the old `obsidian` key are migrated to `brain` exclusively
through `provisionBrain.buildBrainMcpStep`, which emits a
`would-overwrite` step on the next `/aide:upgrade` or `/aide:init`
run. The CLI never silently rewrites a user's `obsidian` key, and no
runtime fallback to the legacy key exists anywhere else in the
codebase. See `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` for the
migration branches.

**Brain vault is required; location is inherent; scaffolding is
silent-on-create.** AIDE is opinionated that the pipeline needs
persistent memory. The consumer asks the user where their vault is,
offering discovered hints (env var, sibling `my-brain/`, prior
install) as suggestions the user can pick or override — the user
cannot skip the vault, only choose where it lives. Once the path is
resolved, the brain category's scaffolding plan follows the normal
file-write branch: if the expected directories are absent, the
install service creates them silently; if the vault exists and the
scaffolding is intact, everything shows `exists` and no writes
happen. The inherent prompt (where?) and the silent-on-create write
(create the directories once the path is known) are cleanly
separated. This closes the outcomes.undesired lines about skipping
the brain and about silently adopting a sibling default.

**Framework detection is a read-only inspection whose result is
always confirmed.** Framework choice is inherent — two frameworks
can coexist on disk, and the user alone knows which one matters for
this project. The detector returns its best guess and the consumer
confirms with the user; the user can accept or override. Detection
never writes. The confirmation prompt happens even when the
detection is unambiguous because the user is the source of truth
about their own project, not the presence of a `.claude/`
directory.

**IDE configuration is per-IDE and always asked.** The user's IDE
preferences are inherent — a developer on VS Code has no use for a
Zed settings file, and vice versa. The consumer asks which IDEs to
configure; it only applies what the user confirms. IDE steps that
need external tooling (VS Code extension install via CLI) return an
`instructions` field the consumer can execute rather than attempting
the shell call from inside the install service, because the install
service has no guarantee the CLI is on PATH and "the rest of the
install succeeds when IDE fails" requires that IDE failure be
reportable, not crashing.

**Network is never the happy path.** Every asset the install action
needs is checked into the repo — the methodology docs, the command
templates, the agent definitions, the skill templates, the bundled
VS Code `.vsix`. A developer running the install on a laptop with no
network gets the same result as a developer running it online, and
the silent-on-create branch is safe because the canonical content
is local and deterministic.

**The upgrade surface must mirror the install surface, category for
category.** Every new install category has a corresponding upgrade
category, and the two lists are maintained together. The
outcomes.undesired line naming drift between `InitCategory` and
`UpgradeCategory` is a deliberate guardrail — a category the install
action installs but upgrade does not track is silently frozen at
install time, and a category upgrade tracks without an install
counterpart is unreachable on cold start. Keep them in lockstep.

**Two consumers, one wire shape per consumer.** The MCP-tool
consumer (`src/tools/init/`) translates the install service's plan
into MCP responses and uses `AskUserQuestion` for inherent-decision
and overwrite prompts. The CLI consumer (`src/cli/init/`) translates
the same plan into terminal output and uses stdin prompts for the
same decisions. Neither consumer is allowed to inline install logic
that the other consumer cannot reach — every category invariant
(idempotency, prescription pattern, planner/apply split, status
enum) lives in the install service, and the consumers are thin
adapters over it. A bug fix in the install service fixes both
consumers; a fix that has to happen in both consumers is a sign the
fix belonged in the service.

## Good examples

**Cold start on a fresh project via the MCP-tool consumer — four
prompts, nothing else.** The agent calls `aide_init` with no
category. The install service returns a plan with every step in
`methodology`, `commands`, `agents`, `skills`, and the `.aide/` tree
as `would-create`; MCP returns its prescription and the brain slot
returns its placeholder because no path is resolved yet; IDE returns
its `would-create` steps.

The agent opens with:

> I detected Claude Code. Is that right?

The user confirms via `AskUserQuestion`. The agent, seeing every file
category is pure `would-create`, applies methodology, commands,
agents, skills, and `.aide/` tree one after another without another
prompt, reporting briefly after each:

> Installed methodology — canonical docs under `.aide/docs/` and a pointer stub in `CLAUDE.md`.
> Installed pipeline slash commands under `.claude/commands/aide/`.
> Installed pipeline agent definitions under `.claude/agents/aide/`.
> Installed study-playbook skill under `.claude/skills/`.
> Scaffolded `.aide/intent.aide` for the project root.

Then the inherent prompts:

> AIDE needs a brain vault. I found a candidate at `D:/Code/Me/my-brain/` (sibling folder) and one at `$BRAIN` (env var → `D:/Notes/main-vault/`). Pick one, or type a path.

The user picks the env-var hint. The agent invokes the install
service with `category: "brain"` and that path; the install service
scaffolds the missing directories and returns `"created"` for each.
Then:

> Your `.mcp.json` has `github` and `memory` configured. I'll add `aide` and `brain` alongside them. OK?

The user confirms; the agent merges and writes. Then:

> I can configure Zed and VS Code for `.aide` preview. Want both, one, or neither?

The user picks Zed only; agent applies the Zed step silently, skips
VS Code. Total prompts from a cold start: four (framework, brain
path, MCP merge, IDE choice). Zero prompts about file-creates.

**Cold start via the npx CLI consumer — same four prompts, terminal
shape.** A developer with no AIDE-aware agent connected runs
`npx aidemd-mcp@latest init` in their project directory. The CLI
invokes the install service in-process and gets back the same plan
the MCP tool would have received. The CLI prints:

> Detected framework: Claude Code. [Y/n]

The developer presses Enter. The CLI walks the file categories
silently (all `would-create`), prints a per-category report after
each apply call, then asks the same brain-vault, MCP-merge, and IDE
prompts via stdin instead of `AskUserQuestion`. Same plan, same
status enum, same idempotency, same outcome on disk — only the wire
shape differs. A developer who runs `aide_init` later from inside a
connected agent's session sees every category as `exists` because
the install service already finished the work.

**Re-run on a fully initialized project — zero prompts, either
consumer.** A week later the developer re-invokes the install
action — could be the agent calling `aide_init`, could be `npx
aidemd-mcp@latest init` from the terminal. Every file category comes
back entirely as `exists`. The MCP config already contains the aide
and brain servers. The brain scaffold is intact. The consumer
reports once and stops:

> This project is already fully initialized — methodology, commands, agents, skills, `.aide` tree, MCP servers, brain vault, and Zed preview all present. Nothing to change.

No prompt fires (no `AskUserQuestion` in the MCP-tool path, no stdin
prompt in the CLI path) because the plan showed nothing to decide.

**Partial overwrite — one file named, one prompt, one category.**
The developer has edited `.claude/commands/aide/research.md` to add
a line of their own instructions. A release later they re-invoke
the install action. The plan comes back with the `commands`
category mixed: every other slash command lands as `would-skip`
(canonical bytes match), and `research.md` lands as
`would-overwrite`. Every other category is pure `would-create` or
pure `exists`.

The consumer applies methodology silently (all `would-create`),
applies agents silently, applies skills silently. At commands it
pauses:

> The `commands` category has one file that would overwrite your existing version: `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`. The other slash commands are either already current or missing and would be freshly installed. Overwrite your modified `research.md`?

Options: "Overwrite it" / "Keep my version and install the others" /
"Skip the whole category." The MCP-tool consumer surfaces these via
`AskUserQuestion`; the CLI consumer surfaces them via a structured
stdin prompt with the same three labels. The user picks the middle
option. The consumer invokes the install service with
`category: "commands"` and signals that the overwrite is declined;
the install service writes only the `would-create` steps, returns
the `would-overwrite` step with status unchanged, and the consumer
reports:

> Installed the other slash commands under `.claude/commands/aide/`. Kept your modified `research.md` as-is.

One prompt, surgically scoped to the one file that actually needed a
decision — same outcome regardless of which consumer drove the run.

## Bad examples

**Prompting on pure-create categories.** Cold start, fresh project,
and the consumer asks:

> Methodology — this will create the CLAUDE.md pointer stub and the canonical methodology docs in `.aide/docs/`. Want me to set this up?

Then:

> Commands — this will create the pipeline slash commands in `.claude/commands/aide/`. Want me to set these up?

Then:

> Agents — this will create the pipeline agent definitions in `.claude/agents/aide/`. Want me to set these up?

The user hits "yes" repeatedly in a row for files that do not yet
exist, staring at a wizard that is asking permission to do the thing
they invoked it to do. This is the exact friction the confirmation
model removes; the category-by-category pause belongs to overwrites,
not creates. Same failure mode whether the consumer asks via
`AskUserQuestion` or via a stdin prompt — the install service's plan
told the consumer there was nothing to decide, and the consumer
asked anyway.

**Silent overwrite because "most of the category is new."** A
developer has a customized `research.md` from the previous example.
The consumer heuristically decides the `commands` category is
"mostly creates (the rest are new, one overwrite)" and applies the
whole category without pausing, overwriting the user's modifications.
The consumer reports "installed the slash commands" and the user
discovers tomorrow that their custom instructions are gone. The
rule is strict: even one `would-overwrite` step in a category tips
the category into the prompt branch.

**Skipping an inherent-decision prompt because the "fast path"
applies.** The consumer sees a `my-brain/` directory sibling to the
project, decides that counts as the brain being "already present,"
skips the brain-path question, and wires the project's MCP config
to that sibling vault. The user's actual AIDE vault is at
`D:/Notes/aide-vault/` and the sibling is an old experiment. The
inherent-decision rule exists precisely for this case: the vault
path is not resolvable from disk state, so the consumer must ask
regardless of what is on disk.

**Silent MCP merge.** The consumer finds `.mcp.json` on disk with
two existing servers, assumes the merge is a file-write decision,
and writes the new config without pausing. In the best case the user
is surprised; in the worst case the existing config was malformed
and the consumer has just replaced it with a "clean" version that
silently dropped the user's custom servers. MCP merge is inherent —
always prompt, in every consumer.

**One summary table, one blanket confirmation, zero granularity.**
The consumer returns:

> Here's the full plan: methodology (creates pending), commands (creates pending), agents (creates pending), skills (create pending), MCP (1 prescription), brain (pending vault path), IDE (creates pending). Apply all?

The user says yes and loses their modified `research.md` to a silent
overwrite they had no way to see in the table. The per-category
pause model exists so overwrites surface against their own category,
with the affected files named — not buried in a global "apply all."

**Prompts fired via free-text instead of structured options.** The
consumer writes:

> Do you want me to go ahead with methodology? (yes/no)

The user types "y" and the consumer parses it. This violates the
structured-options rule from outcomes.undesired — every pause point
must use defined labels (`AskUserQuestion` in the MCP-tool consumer,
structured stdin prompts in the CLI consumer), because free-text
prompts cost the agent a parse turn and the CLI a fragile string
match. Both consumers have native structured-prompt mechanisms; the
install service's invariant is that the consumer uses them.

**Reporting that does not distinguish created from overwritten.**
After an apply call that wrote several new files and overwrote one,
the consumer reports:

> Done — installed all files.

The user has no way to know which file was theirs and got replaced.
The manifest statuses exist so the consumer can say "created N,
overwrote 1: research.md" — collapsing them into a single
undifferentiated count defeats the purpose of the status enum.

**A consumer that inlines install logic instead of going through
the service.** A maintainer adds a "post-install hook" feature to
the MCP-tool consumer that touches files outside the install
service's plan — say, optimistically running `npm install` after the
install action finishes. The CLI consumer doesn't get the hook, the
two consumers diverge, and a future developer running the CLI sees
different results than a developer using the agent. The rule is
that every category invariant lives in the install service; if a
behavior should affect both consumers, it belongs in the service,
not in either consumer.

## References

- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/.aide/intent.aide` — Canonical parent: the
  two delivery channels (handshake teaching + install action) and
  the rule that no doctrine lives outside the canonical docs.
  Anchors the "MCP is a renderer, docs are the source" framing used
  in the Strategy.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/.aide` — Immediate parent: the client-
  parity, idempotency-as-invariant, and "tool never touches MCP
  config" rules the Strategy inherits without restating. Source of
  the per-category idempotency point and the MCP prescription
  contract.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/.aide/docs/aide-spec.md` — Canonical methodology.
  Source of the "intent governs, strategy is decisions with citations,
  no code in the body" rules that shape how this Strategy is written;
  also the "orchestrator/helper, `.aide` next to orchestrators only"
  placement rule that underwrites the category-isolation decision.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/.aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md` — Source of the
  "helper does one thing, orchestrator coordinates" convention the
  category-isolation decision rests on; also the tier model the
  methodology-split context paragraph invokes.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/plan.aide` — Prior
  apply-mode plan. Established the two-call pattern (summary call →
  category apply call), the `"created"` status, and the MCP/IDE
  exceptions. The new `"overwritten"` status slots into the same
  manifest shape without restructuring the call contract.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/todo.aide` — Prior QA
  re-alignment. Documents the "plan scope blindspot" lesson
  (type-change steps must be explicit) and the untested
  passthrough gap; both inform the "manifest statuses must
  distinguish created from overwritten" rule and the category-
  isolation invariant.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/detectFramework/index.ts` —
  Source of the per-framework path table (`configPath`,
  `commandDir`, `docHubDir`, etc.). Confirms that disk inspection
  alone cannot resolve framework choice when multiple frameworks'
  markers coexist, anchoring the "framework is an inherent
  decision" point.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/installAgents/plan.aide` —
  Sibling category plan. Establishes the agents/skills delivery
  shape the silent-on-create branch applies to, and confirms that
  the install and upgrade category lists must move in lockstep.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/tools/init/` — MCP-tool consumer that
  exposes the install service through the `aide_init` MCP tool.
  Translates plan/apply calls into MCP responses and uses
  `AskUserQuestion` for inherent-decision and overwrite prompts.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/cli/init/` — npx CLI consumer that
  exposes the install service through `npx aidemd-mcp@latest init`.
  Drives the same plan/apply boundary in-process and uses stdin
  prompts for inherent-decision and overwrite branches.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/.claude/commands/aide/init.md` — Wizard
  prompt for the MCP-tool consumer. Source of the `AskUserQuestion`
  contract and the "never summary table, never 'all' option" rules
  referenced in the Bad examples.
